    ("host", "Okay, give me an example. What's the first thing you look at when a cut feels wrong?"),
    ("guest", "Honestly? The pauses. Most rough cuts have too much air. Someone finishes a sentence, and there's a full second of nothing before the next person speaks. On set it felt natural. On screen it feels like the energy just leaks out."),
    ("host", "So you tighten the gaps."),
    ("guest", "I tighten the gaps. Not all of them, though. If you remove every pause, people sound like robots. You keep the pauses that mean something. A breath before a big answer. A beat after a joke."),
    ("host", "That's the hard part, isn't it? Knowing which silence is doing work."),
    ("guest", "Exactly. And the waveform helps. You can literally see the shape of a conversation. Big blocks are speech, flat lines are silence, and little spikes are clicks and breaths."),
    ("host", "Let's talk about music for a second. A lot of people put a track under everything, start to finish."),
    ("guest", "And then they wonder why the voice is hard to hear. You have to duck the music. When someone talks, the music goes down, maybe twelve or fifteen decibels. When they stop, it comes back up. Smoothly, not like a switch."),
